<div class="three-tool-facts"><div><span>Quranic examples</span><strong>5 referenced duas</strong></div><div><span>Sunnah reports</span><strong>Morning and after-Salah</strong></div><div><span>Ruling</span><strong>No invented counts</strong></div></div>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-meaning"><h3>What are Adhkar and Duas?</h3><p><em>Dhikr</em> refers to remembering Allah and <em>dua</em> to supplication. They overlap but are not identical labels. Quranic verses can contain supplications, while narrated Sunnah may describe specific words, settings, numbers and benefits. <a href="https://quran.com/13/28">Quran 13:28</a> connects remembrance of Allah with the tranquillity of hearts. Our app clearly separates text taken from Quranic verses from cards that point to authentic hadith reports.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-quran"><h3>Five Quranic supplications with sources</h3><p>The collection includes the plea for knowledge (<a href="https://quran.com/20/114">20:114</a>), good in this world and the next (<a href="https://quran.com/2/201">2:201</a>), steadfast hearts (<a href="https://quran.com/3/8">3:8</a>), the supplication of Yunus (<a href="https://quran.com/21/87">21:87</a>), and mercy for parents (<a href="https://quran.com/17/24">17:24</a>). The on-page Arabic and short meaning are excerpts and explanations; click each source to inspect the Quranic context and translation.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-morning"><h3>Morning and evening Sunnah Adhkar</h3><p>Reported daily remembrance can include reading Al-Ikhlas, Al-Falaq and An-Nas at morning and evening; see <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:5082">Sunan Abi Dawud 5082</a>. The description, exact count and grading should be read in context rather than assigned indiscriminately to every Quranic dua. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:6306">Sahih al-Bukhari 6306</a> also reports the well-known Sayyid al-Istighfar supplication with timing and virtue. The tool links to these complete texts instead of supplying a partial unsourced Arabic rendition.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-salah"><h3>Dhikr after Salah and the 33/33 patterns</h3><p>Among the transmitted practices is the after-prayer remembrance of <em>SubhanAllah</em>, <em>Alhamdulillah</em> and <em>Allahu Akbar</em> in reported counts. <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:597a">Sahih Muslim 597a</a> describes 33 repetitions of each followed by a final Tawhid declaration. Other authentic reports provide alternate patterns. The <a href="/tools/tasbih-counter/">Tasbih Counter</a> offers separate selectable variants rather than pretending they are all the same.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-plan"><h3>How to build a manageable daily remembrance habit</h3><ol><li>Choose an established authentic source appropriate to the time or occasion.</li><li>Read its Arabic and understand the meaning as well as you can.</li><li>Follow any reported count only when the particular narration supports it.</li><li>Keep the core routine manageable and consistent, without burdening yourself with dozens of unverified claims.</li><li>Use the optional reading tally only as a personal aid, not proof of religious merit.</li><li>For Arabic pronunciation and wording seek a reliable teacher or recitation source.</li></ol></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-example"><h3>Worked example: a morning and after-Salah remembrance routine</h3><div class="three-tool-example"><p>A reader chooses <strong>Quran 20:114</strong> for an unscheduled personal supplication; the app does not set a mandatory repetition count for it. Separately, after an obligatory Salah, the reader may follow the reported <strong>33 SubhanAllah, 33 Alhamdulillah and 33 Allahu Akbar plus final declaration</strong> from Sahih Muslim 597a.</p><p>These are different categories: the first is a Quranic dua available for personal recitation, while the second is an occasion-specific narrated format. Avoid describing either as a full replacement for the complete daily Sunnah.</p></div></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-mistakes"><h3>How to recognise authentic Adhkar and avoid fabricated rewards</h3><p>Verify exact wording, Arabic vowels, the hadith reference and its assessment, the context (morning, evening, after prayer, travel or sleep), and whether a stated number is actually transmitted. A social-media image that promises a precise worldly reward without evidence is not sufficient. Quran.com and numbered hadith collections let readers trace a statement rather than trusting a paraphrase.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="adhkar-guide-privacy"><h3>Reading controls, Arabic text and privacy</h3><p>The Quranic dua reading counter is intentionally temporary: changing entries resets it, and it does not create an account or store a sensitive worship history. The app provides brief English meanings, which are not substitutes for a comprehensive tafsir. Follow the linked verses for the full wording and meaning.</p></section>
